@@ -24,3 +24,35 @@ changes which posts and translations the deployed build carries.
 Redeploy the previous commit's build. There is no database to migrate back —
 the post catalog and everything else the app reads live in the build itself —
 so a rollback here is just a redeploy.
+
+### Confirming it worked
+
+- Your deploy platform's dashboard shows the redeployed build as live for
+  Production, not just queued or building.
+- The home page loads and its post list matches what the previous commit
+  shipped — if the build you're rolling back away from added, edited, or
+  removed a post, that change should now be reversed, since post content is
+  baked into the build rather than read from a database.
+- Open a post that has more than one translation and confirm it renders the
+  locale you expect (see [Locale fallback](#locale-fallback) above), not a
+  blank page. A rollback that only reverts code but serves a stale or
+  half-built bundle tends to show up here first.
+- The specific symptom that made you roll back is actually gone, not just
+  quieter.
+
+### After it's confirmed
+
+This project has no error monitoring or alerting wired up, so there's no
+dashboard to watch — recheck the page(s) that showed the original symptom a
+few minutes later and once more after about an hour, since some failure
+modes (bad caching, a translation that only fails for one locale) don't show
+up on the first load. Leave a short note — a GitHub issue is fine — recording
+what broke, when you rolled back, and the commit you rolled back to; that
+note is what the eventual fix-forward pull request should link back to.
+
+### Who needs to know
+
+Tell whoever else has deploy access or reviews pull requests on this project
+that a rollback happened, why, and which commit is now live — before they
+push their next change on top of what they'd otherwise assume was the
+current `main`.
